  "title": "St. Martin's Croissants and Pyry z Gzikiem",
  "lead": "Greater Poland's cuisine is potatoes in every form, croissants with white poppy seeds, and goose for St. Martin's Day. Slow travel through Greater Poland cuisine.",
  "body": "<h2>Why Greater Poland cuisine, differently</h2><p>Greater Poland is a region where potatoes — called <em>pyry</em> here — reign. Pyry z gzikiem (with curd cheese and onion), pyry with sour cream, pyry with bacon, potato pancakes — all dishes that appear on Greater Poland tables almost daily. Greater Poland cuisine is agricultural cuisine — hearty, substantial, based on what the land gives. It is one of the most \"homey\" culinary regions of Poland.</p><p>Slow travel through Greater Poland cuisine is a celebration of taste and tradition. Instead of rushing from restaurant to restaurant, stop in one longer — in a village inn, in a milk bar, in an agritourism farm. Each dish has its own story, its own cook, its own village. Ask where the ingredients come from, how the dish is prepared, who passed down the recipe. This is a journey that rewards those who slow down — the longer you are in one place, the more flavors you can truly get to know.</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Greater Poland cuisine splits into several categories: potato dishes (pyry z gzikiem, pancakes, kopytka), meat dishes (goose for St. Martin's Day, duck), baked goods (St. Martin's croissant, szneki z glancem), flour dishes (dumplings, pierogi), liqueurs (kruszonka). Exploring the culinary region of Greater Poland takes 2–3 days.</p><h3>Potato dishes</h3><ul><li><strong>Pyry z gzikiem</strong> — potatoes in their jackets with curd cheese, onion, and sour cream. One of the most characteristic dishes of the region — simple, filling, and deeply rooted in Greater Poland tradition.</li><li><strong>Potato pancakes</strong> — in the Greater Poland version with marjoram and bacon. Served with sour cream or mushroom sauce.</li><li><strong>Kopytka</strong> — potato dumplings in an elongated shape, served with sauce, meat, or as a standalone dish.</li><li><strong>Pyry with sour cream</strong> — potatoes in their jackets with sour cream and chives. A simple, classic dish.</li></ul><h3>Meat dishes and baked goods</h3><ul><li><strong>Goose for St. Martin's Day</strong> — roast goose with modra kapusta and dumplings. The classic St. Martin's dinner, which appears in Greater Poland every year on 11 November. Goose meat is fatty but delicate.</li><li><strong>Duck with apples</strong> — roast duck with apples and marjoram, a Greater Poland classic. In the rural version — with caraway and garlic.</li><li><strong>St. Martin's croissant</strong> — the icon of Poznań. A sweet croissant with white poppy seeds, dried fruit, and almonds, baked only on 11 November (though today available in pastry shops throughout the year). The tradition dates back to the 19th century, the product is EU-protected.</li><li><strong>Szneki z glancem</strong> — sweet yeast buns with streusel and icing. Popular for breakfast throughout Greater Poland.</li><li><strong>Greater Poland kołocz</strong> — sweet yeast cake with streusel and various additions, baked for weddings and celebrations.</li></ul><h3>Other specialties</h3><ul><li><strong>Greater Poland krupnik</strong> — barley groat soup with vegetables and meat. Hearty, warming.</li><li><strong>Greater Poland żur</strong> — sour rye soup with white sausage and egg. A Greater Poland classic.</li><li><strong>Potato sausage (kiszka ziemniaczana)</strong> — sausage made of potato mass with bacon, baked in the oven. Served with onion and mustard.</li><li><strong>Kruszonka</strong> — Greater Poland grain liqueur, available in local shops.</li><li><strong>Korycin cheese</strong> — ripening cheese from Korycin, smoked or natural. Inscribed on the EU protected products list.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>April–June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Fewer tourists, fresh seasonal produce, long days. Worth a walk through markets.</li><li><strong>July–September</strong> — season for fresh vegetables, fruit. Worth it for markets and culinary festivals.</li><li><strong>September–November</strong> — season for goose (St. Martin's Day, 11 November), St. Martin's croissants, pumpkins. Worth it for culinary events.</li><li><strong>November–March</strong> — season for bigos, żur, krupnik. Worth it for winter cuisine, especially during the holiday season.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><p>Greater Poland cuisine is from the western region of Poland — best to travel by car or train, connecting several towns in one trip.</p><ul><li><strong>By train:</strong> Poznań, Leszno, Kalisz, Gniezno have railway stations, with connections from most Polish cities.</li><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~3 hours from Warsaw (S8 and A2), ~3 hours from Berlin (A2), ~2 hours from Wrocław (S5 and S11).</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS and private operators serve most towns on the route.</li></ul><h2>How much time you need</h2><p>A weekend (2 days) is the minimum — Poznań (St. Martin's croissant, pyry z gzikiem) and surroundings.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Poznań, Rogalin, Kórnik, Gniezno.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore the whole culinary region of Greater Poland — all the most important dishes and places.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><p>Instead of restaurants, choose milk bars and village inns. There, cooks prepare what they have known since childhood, and they do it best.</p><ul><li><strong>St. Martin's croissant</strong> — the icon of Poznań, available at pastry shops throughout the city.</li><li><strong>Pyry z gzikiem</strong> — a Greater Poland classic, available at milk bars and inns.</li><li><strong>Goose for St. Martin's Day</strong> — seasonally, from November, available in restaurants in Poznań.</li><li><strong>Szneki z glancem</strong> — sweet buns, available at bakeries in Poznań.</li><li><strong>Korycin cheese</strong> — ripening cheese, available in local shops.</li><li><strong>Beer from local breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region (Browar Szałpiw, Browar Kormoran).</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Poznań (largest choice, more options), Leszno (town, more options), Kalisz (town, more options), Gniezno (town, smaller choice), agritourism farms in villages (quiet, home-cooked food).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Milk bars</strong> — usually open from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m., often closed on weekends. Check hours before your trip.</li><li><strong>Regional restaurants</strong> — look for places where cooks prepare traditional recipes. Avoid tourist restaurants in city centres.</li><li><strong>Culinary workshops</strong> — many farms and agritourism businesses offer workshops (baking croissants, making gzikiem, baking bread). Book ahead.</li><li><strong>St. Martin's croissant</strong> — in November, book croissants in advance. In other months, available at many pastry shops.</li><li><strong>Markets</strong> — the best place to buy local products (cheeses, honey, cold cuts, croissants).</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — weather in the region can be changeable. Even in July, bring a rain jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good in towns, weaker in villages. Have an offline map.</li></ul>",
